A datastorage with no attributes of its own aborts the whole extraction.

UACFinder.start_search iterates every datastorage the importer registered and calls
_find_UACs on it, which asks get_max_entry_number for the largest entry number among
its attributes. That builds `... WHERE attribute_id IN ({combination_string})`, and for
an empty attribute list the string is empty, so the statement reads `IN ()`. MariaDB
rejects it, DBConnector.query prints the error and returns None, and the caller
subscripts None:

    TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not subscriptable

and the job goes to `aborted`. Nothing downstream runs -- no INDs, no keys, no
references -- so the failure is total rather than partial.

A nested document path whose children are all objects is enough to trigger it. The
importer registers such a path as a datastorage (it has a parent_id and its own
children) but records no attribute against it, because it has no scalar leaf of its
own. UniBench happens to contain no such path. MMSBench-Net's log documents contain
two, so the extractor cannot run on that corpus at all as shipped.

The guard below is the reading the code already assumes everywhere else: a datastorage
with no attributes has no unique attribute combination, so there is nothing to search
and the search returns immediately. It changes no result on any input that did not
previously crash, which is why it is safe to use for a baseline comparison -- without
it there would be no baseline to compare against.

--- a/services/UniqueAttributecombinations.py
+++ b/services/UniqueAttributecombinations.py
@@
         attributes_to_remove = []

+        # A datastorage with no attributes -- a nested document path whose children are
+        # all objects -- has no unique attribute combination to find. Without this the
+        # empty list reaches get_max_entry_number and becomes `IN ()`, which is a syntax
+        # error, and the job aborts before any dependency is reported.
+        if not attributes:
+            return
+
         # Determine number of entries
         number_of_entries = self.connector.get_max_entry_number(attributes)
